WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.460
<v Instructor>Okay, so let's get into DynamoDB.</v>

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.310
So DynamoDB is going to be a NoSQL database,

3
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.430
fully managed, serverless, and can provide massive scale.

4
00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.030
It can go up to 1 million requests per second.

5
00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.710
It's going to have a similar architecture

6
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.910
to Apache Casandra,

7
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:19.830
and users of Cassandra can migrate to DynamoDB, for example.

8
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.930
There's no disk space to provision,

9
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.960
and the max object size is going to be 400 kilobytes.

10
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.810
And a way to store bigger objects into DynamoDB

11
00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.420
is to actually store the objects into Amazon S3

12
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.110
and store a reference to that object in DynamoDB.

13
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.060
In terms of capacity, you have two modes.

14
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.170
You have provision capacity,

15
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.810
where you specify how many write capacity units

16
00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.900
and read capacity units and if you want autoscaling on.

17
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.390
Or you can use on-demand, where you're going to have to pay

18
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.917
for every single write and read.

19
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:49.080
And this is better when you're going to have

20
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.470
a more unexpected, unpredictable type of workload.

21
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:53.760
So provision is going to give you

22
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.460
the smoothest type of scaling and a better cost,

23
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.080
whereas on-demand is gonna be very helpful

24
00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.910
if you don't know your patterns in advance,

25
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.670
or you're doing some development, et cetera, et cetera.

26
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.640
It supports CRUD, so Create, Read, Update, Delete.

27
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.520
And the read can be eventually or strongly consistent.

28
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.040
And it supports transactions across multiple tables.

29
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.550
So it is possible to get ACID support with DynamoDB.

30
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.700
You get backups available and point-in-time recovery.

31
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.830
And finally, you have two table classes,

32
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.510
standard for frequently accessed data

33
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.380
and infrequent access for infrequently accessed data.

34
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.390
So everything as said, you should already know,

35
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.340
but let's go back over the basics real quick.

36
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.500
DynamoDB is made of tables.

37
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.210
And each table has primary keys

38
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.210
that must be decided at creation time.

39
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.070
Each table can have an infinite number of items,

40
00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.040
which are called rows, and each item will have attributes.

41
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.500
They're like columns that can be added over time,

42
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.730
and they can be null.

43
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.820
As I said, an item cannot be bigger than 400 kilobytes.

44
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.140
And in terms of data types supported,

45
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.010
you have scalar, so string, number, binary, Boolean, null.

46
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.170
You get document types such list, map.

47
00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.910
And you get set types such as string set,

48
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.040
number set, and binary set.

49
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.810
Okay, primary key is more important.

50
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.600
So option one, you get just a partition key,

51
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.800
and it's called a HASH.

52
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.870
And in this case, the partition key

53
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.820
must be unique for each item.

54
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.640
The whole idea behind the primary key is the uniqueness.

55
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.070
And so the partition key must be diverse enough

56
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.170
so that the data is going to be distributed.

57
00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.730
For example, if you have a users table,

58
00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.000
user_id is going to be a great partition key.

59
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.230
So we'll have the partition key,

60
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.640
and then we'll have a bunch of attributes

61
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.530
such as First Name and Age.

62
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.420
And this gives us the kind of users like this.

63
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.400
So for John, there will be a user_id,

64
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.260
and for Katie, another user_id.

65
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.820
And so this user_id is great.

66
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.410
We only have one. It's unique.

67
00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.900
So it's a great choice for a partition key.

68
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.390
The other option is to use a partition key

69
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.740
that's going to be a primary key

70
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.060
that's going to be composites.

71
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.570
And so it's called a partition key plus a sort key,

72
00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.070
in which case what must be unique

73
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.960
is the combination of the two.

74
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.450
So the partition key itself can be repeated

75
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.220
as long as the sort key associated with it

76
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.020
is going to be different every time.

77
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.300
The data is going to be grouped logically

78
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.310
on the tables by partition key.

79
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.290
And it's also common to see the sort key

80
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.030
being called a range key.

81
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.190
For example, if you have users-and-games table,

82
00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.530
user_id would be great for the partition key,

83
00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.740
and game_id would be great for the sort key.

84
00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.170
So it's possible that a user has multiple games,

85
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.240
but each game_id and user_id combination

86
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.790
is going to be unique, hence this is a great choice.

87
00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.720
Another good choice for a sort key is usually timestamp.

88
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.920
So you're trying to describe something

89
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.670
that happens over time for a specific user_id and so on.

90
00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.710
You will choose user_id for partition key,

91
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.680
and you will choose a timestamp column for the sort key.

92
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.870
So here's the example for user_id and game_id.

93
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.520
We have the partition key and the sort key.

94
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.730
And together, as a composite key, they are unique,

95
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.010
and the result can be some of the attributes.

96
00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.660
So again, here's an example,

97
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.500
for this user_id, 12 something 45.

98
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.030
Then there's two games that have been played.

99
00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.740
One was won, and one was lost.

100
00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.420
And so we can see that the combination

101
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.810
of the user_id and the game_id in this case is unique.

102
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.600
Okay, hopefully all of this is not new to you.

103
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.130
Then we have indexes.

104
00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.680
Object is made of a partition key,

105
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.380
a sort key that is optional, and some attributes.

106
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.990
And so we can define some indexes on top of it.

107
00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.980
So the first one is an LSI, or Local Secondary Index.

108
00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.650
You need to make sure the partition key is kept.

109
00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.070
And you will just select an alternative sort key.

110
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.650
And this LSI, or Local Secondary Index,

111
00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.660
must be defined at table creation time.

112
00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.060
It cannot be defined afterwards.

113
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.410
The other index you have

114
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.630
is in case you want to change the primary key.

115
00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.900
Then it's called a GSI for Global Secondary Index.

116
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.970
In this case, you can change the primary key,

117
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.490
and you can also optionally have a sort key.

118
00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.820
And it can be defined after the table is created.

119
00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.240
So the idea in DynamoDB is that you can only query

120
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.920
by primary key plus sort key

121
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.800
on the main table and the indexes.

122
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.970
So it's not like RDS,

123
00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.450
where you can query any single column and any single rows.

124
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.430
In DynamoDB, you need to think about

125
00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.410
what your queries are going to be

126
00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.840
and create the right indexes for these queries

127
00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:58.680
for it to work.

128
00:04:58.680 --> 00:04:59.970
So this is a major difference

129
00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.360
between a traditional RDBMS such as RDS

130
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.730
and a NoSQL database such as DynamoDB.

131
00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:09.120
In our case, in DynamoDB, if you want to query something,

132
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.650
you need to make sure an index

133
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.750
has been created for it in advance.

134
00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:17.040
Now, DynamoDB is a point of focus on the Developer exam,

135
00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.380
little less on the Solutions Architect Pro.

136
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:20.430
So you don't need to know all the details,

137
00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.620
but remember, between the LSI and the GSI,

138
00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.660
but remember that you need an index

139
00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.360
if you want to query a specific attribute in DynamoDB.

140
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.600
Okay, so some important features of DynamoDB.

141
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.840
Number one is TTL, which is to make a row expire

142
00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.000
after a specific amount of time.

143
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.670
We have DynamoDB Streams where we want to react

144
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.590
to changes in DynamoDB tables in real time.

145
00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.600
So this is a very common architecture,

146
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:43.890
and this stream can be read

147
00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.650
by Lambda, very commonly, or EC2.

148
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.530
You get 24 hours retention of data in that stream.

149
00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.630
So here's a very common architecture.

150
00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.330
You have Create, Read, Update, Deletes are done to a table

151
00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.710
and anything that modifies the table.

152
00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.750
So Create, Update, and Delete

153
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.390
will be put into a DynamoDB stream.

154
00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.520
And that stream can be read by Lambda.

155
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.230
And then from Lambda, you can, for example,

156
00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.170
put the data into Amazon OpenSearch,

157
00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.900
or can Kinesis if you want it to have some downstream,

158
00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.330
maybe Kinesis analytics application on top of it.

159
00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.750
So this is a very, very common solution architecture.

160
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.460
We also have global tables,

161
00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.730
which is basically a table that is going to be replicated

162
00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.290
across all your regions.

163
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.270
And this is Active-Active type of replication,

164
00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.860
so you can have many regions.

165
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.320
And for this, you need to enable DynamoDB Streams first.

166
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.900
And it's very useful if you want to have low latency access

167
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.850
to data into different regions,

168
00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.310
or you want to create a disaster recovery strategy

169
00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.860
that's going to have a very, very low RTO.

170
00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.000
So here's the example.

171
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.490
We have two tables, one in us-east-1

172
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.130
and the other one in ap-southeast-2.

173
00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.950
And whatever we do to the table on us-east-1

174
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.250
is going to be replicated to the table in ap-southeast-2.

175
00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.590
And similarly, whatever we do in ap-southeast-2

176
00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.833
is going to be replicated back into us-east-1.

177
00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.920
Okay, you can use can Kinesis Data Streams

178
00:06:58.920 --> 00:06:59.970
with DynamoDB Streams.

179
00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:00.900
How does that work?

180
00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.060
Well, we know that DynamoDB Streams

181
00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.980
will capture item-level changes,

182
00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.960
but we can now send these changes

183
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.050
directly into a desired Kinesis data stream.

184
00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:10.883
Why would you do that?

185
00:07:10.883 --> 00:07:13.320
Well, we only have 24 hours of data retention

186
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.490
in DynamoDB Streams.

187
00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:15.870
But with Kinesis Data Streams,

188
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.220
we can get longer data retention period up to 365 days.

189
00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.190
Also, we could use the Kinesis-related tool suites

190
00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.510
to analyze the data.

191
00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.343
So how does that work?

192
00:07:25.343 --> 00:07:27.690
So DynamoDB tables will push item-level changes

193
00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.660
in Kinesis data stream, and then we can use, for example,

194
00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.120
Kinesis Data Firehose to read this data

195
00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.250
and store it into the usual places,

196
00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.000
so Amazon S3, Redshift, or OpenSearch.

197
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.470
Or you could use Kinesis Data Analytics

198
00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.390
to analyze this data in real time

199
00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.920
and do some real-time computation

200
00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.410
such as some filtering, aggregation, transformation,

201
00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.470
and send that later on to Kinesis Data Streams,

202
00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.960
Kinesis Data Firehose, or Lambda.

203
00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.450
So now let's get into solution architecture.

204
00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.510
So we know that Amazon S3 is not a great way

205
00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.470
to search for objects,

206
00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.080
because it doesn't have any indexing capabilities.

207
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.490
And so what we wanna do

208
00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:06.450
is store some metadata information into DynamoDB.

209
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.370
So the way it works is that we will do writes

210
00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:12.090
to Amazon S3 buckets to add objects or update objects,

211
00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.290
and we'll have a Lambda function

212
00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.170
being triggered by the S3 events.

213
00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.150
This Lambda function is going to take the metadata

214
00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.060
of the objects we're trying to insert into Amazon S3

215
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.670
and insert this into a DynamoDB table.

216
00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.670
And the cool thing about it is that now we can create an API

217
00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:30.239
to search for object metadata by creating the right indexes,

218
00:08:30.239 --> 00:08:32.070
the LSI and the GSI.

219
00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.220
So some things we can do thanks to this architecture

220
00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:36.540
is now we can say, okay,

221
00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.890
give me all the objects by date in Amazon S3.

222
00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.690
Or give me the total amount of storage

223
00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.580
used by a certain customer.

224
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.880
Or give me all the objects that have a certain attribute.

225
00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.190
So by having these objects into S3 only,

226
00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.650
we could never answer these kind of queries.

227
00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.540
By having it into a DynamoDB table,

228
00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.073
it becomes quite trivial.

229
00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.030
So finally, let's talk about caching in DynamoDB.

230
00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.970
So DAX, as you may know, is DynamoDB Accelerator.

231
00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.550
And it is a seamless cache for DynamoDB.

232
00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.410
You don't need to rewrite your application.

233
00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.050
And all the writes we go through DAX to DynamoDB.

234
00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.780
And what this gives you is micro-second latency

235
00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.810
for cache reads and queries, which is very handy.

236
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.190
It solves the hot key problem.

237
00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.960
So if there is, in DynamoDB,

238
00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.330
a specific row that is being read too many times

239
00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:22.890
because it is very popular,

240
00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.590
then you're gonna get a throttling issue in DynamoDB,

241
00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.140
in which case, creating a DAX cluster

242
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.100
in front of the DynamoDB

243
00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.020
is going to solve that problem for you

244
00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.090
by caching that row and making sure

245
00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.490
it doesn't ever reach DynamoDB.

246
00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.040
By default, your data is going to be cached

247
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.400
for five minutes in your DAX, and there will be the TTL.

248
00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.130
Then the data will have to be re-fetched from DynamoDB.

249
00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.620
You can get up to 10 nodes in the cluster.

250
00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.098
And it could be multi-AZ, in which case,

251
00:09:49.098 --> 00:09:52.320
AWS recommends you three nodes minimum for production.

252
00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.153
It's also secure.

253
00:09:53.153 --> 00:09:55.650
You get encryption at rest with KMS, VPC integration,

254
00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:57.630
IAM, CloudTrail, and so on.

255
00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.660
And this is a default choice for a cache in DynamoDB.

256
00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:02.850
But we'll have a bit of a discussion

257
00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.770
into the right strategy right after.

258
00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.080
So the idea is your application wants to write

259
00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.240
to DynamoDB tables and read from them,

260
00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.220
and in the middle is going to be DAX.

261
00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.750
And it's gonna be seamless for us.

262
00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.460
And it's going to cache the right rows

263
00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:17.640
and make sure we get the right performance where we need it.

264
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.160
So the question is, from an architectural point of view,

265
00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.880
should you use DAX or ElastiCache for DynamoDB?

266
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:28.140
So as a very simple problem, when you have your clients

267
00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:30.720
who want to directly access DynamoDB,

268
00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.000
then the right answer is to use DAX.

269
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.590
Because you will scan,

270
00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.140
you will have the individual objects

271
00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.360
being cached in DAX directly,

272
00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.160
and even the results of your query

273
00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.010
and your scan will be in DAX.

274
00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.020
But let's assume that your client,

275
00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.570
after retrieving a lot of the data,

276
00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.790
performs some heavy computation.

277
00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.520
And this computation can take, I don't know,

278
00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:54.810
10 seconds, 30 seconds.

279
00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.430
It can take a long time.

280
00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:00.120
Then you will want to be able to cache the results

281
00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.340
of these computations somewhere.

282
00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.620
And that's somewhere may be ElastiCache.

283
00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.100
So for example, ElastiCache index example could be a place

284
00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.010
where you want to store your aggregation results

285
00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.950
and make sure other clients can look into ElastiCache

286
00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:16.830
before getting data from DynamoDB

287
00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.200
to get the aggregation results.

288
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.000
So it is one kind of architecture.

289
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.850
You could also swap ElastiCache for DynamoDB itself

290
00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.910
and have a table act as a cache, it is possible.

291
00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:30.060
But I want to show you here that DAX and ElastiCache

292
00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:32.310
can serve very different purposes, okay?

293
00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.310
DAX is going to be for individual object cache

294
00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:38.340
and for query and scan caches in DynamoDB,

295
00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.430
whereas ElastiCache is for whatever you wanna put in it.

296
00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.540
So it's very possible for you

297
00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.400
to perform some heavy computations

298
00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.560
and cache them centrally in the ElastiCache

299
00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.170
to avoid going back to DAX and DynamoDB

300
00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.960
and do some expensive computations again.

301
00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.670
Okay, so that's it into DynamoDB.

302
00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:55.530
Remember, it's from the architectural point of view.

303
00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:57.270
This is all you need to know for the exam.

304
00:11:57.270 --> 00:11:59.220
And I will see you in the next lecture.

